@meta
id: 27-03
title: Put it back the way you found it
chapter: 27
type: lesson
skills: set-option
par: 51
minutes: 5
vscode: nothing — VS Code has no word-character setting to break
open: order.repository.ts
vimrc: set iskeyword-=_

@brief
Somebody in this session has been clever with `iskeyword`.

`iskeyword` is the list of characters vim counts as part of a word. It is
what `w`, `*`, `diw` and `ciw` all consult, and by default it includes the
underscore — which is why `ciw` on `customer_id` changes the whole
identifier and not just the first half of it.

Here it has had the underscore taken out. Every word motion in this buffer
now stops at an underscore, and `ciw` on `customer_id` will give you
`buyer_id` when you wanted `buyer`.

Rename `customer_id` to `buyer` in both places. Get the option back to
vim's own default first — you are not guessing at a value here, you are
asking vim to forget the change.

@keys
| `:set iskeyword&` | reset an option to vim's built-in default |
| `:set iskeyword?` | show what it is holding now |
| `:set iskeyword-=_` | remove an item from a list option |
| `:set all&` | reset every option — the panic button |

@hints
Your word motions are lying to you. Fix the option before you touch the
text; do it the other way round and you will be repairing two identifiers
by hand.
--
`&` after an option name resets it. `:verbose set iskeyword?` will also
tell you the value it currently holds.
--
`:set iskeyword&`, then `/customer_id` `Enter`, `ciwbuyer` `Esc`, then
`n` and `.` for the second one.

@solution
    :set iskeyword&<Enter>  /customer_id<Enter>  ciwbuyer<Esc>  n.  :wq<Enter>

Forty-four keystrokes.

`&` is the third thing you can do to an option, after setting it and
asking about it: `:set opt&` throws away whatever anyone did to it and
restores vim's compiled-in default.

Read that last sentence again, because it is the trap. `&` restores
*vim's* default, not *your* default. Your `.vimrc` says `shiftwidth=2`;
vim's built-in default is 8. `:set shiftwidth&` leaves you on 8, which is
almost certainly not what you meant. There is `:set opt&vim` for "the
default vim would use in nocompatible mode", and there is no form at all
that means "whatever my config said" — for that you re-source your config.

`&` is at its best exactly where it was used here: an option you did not
set, whose correct value you do not remember, that something has bent out
of shape. `iskeyword`, `formatoptions`, `comments` and `path` all qualify.
For anything your own config sets on purpose, type the value.

`:set all&` resets everything at once. Keep it in mind for the day a
half-loaded plugin leaves you in a vim that does not behave.

@start order.repository.ts
export class OrderRepository {
  public findByCustomer(customer_id: string) {
    return this.prisma.order.findMany({ where: { customer_id } });
  }
}

@expected order.repository.ts
export class OrderRepository {
  public findByCustomer(buyer: string) {
    return this.prisma.order.findMany({ where: { buyer } });
  }
}
